\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\sl-827\slmult0\keepn\nowidctlpar\pvpara\dropcapli3\dropcapt1\faroman\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13186552 {\fs112\dn11\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 T
\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx204\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 HIRTY years ago it \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
was considered almost a duty for an ambitious performer to find interesting points of his own in well-known classical works. It was a time when con\-
ductors indulged in all sorts of rubatos, sudden pianissimos or fortissimos, surprisingly accentuated inner\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 parts, revisions of orches\-
tration. The general opinion seems to have been that a composer as a rule was too lazy or too secluded from the world or simply too uninterested to care for such trifles, and that a conscientious per\-former had to supply all the
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
nuances which he omitted through negligence or inexperience or because of the imperfection of instruments at his time. Today we are enjoying another fashion, that of orthodox accuracy. Oddly enough one can get as many new and surprising aspects of things
\hich\f0 \emdash \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 a\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 nd sometimes as much nonsense\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
by dogmatic fidelity to the letter as by its opposite. Musical notation is nothing more than a scanty sketch of the music itself, a system of conventional signs, the meaning of which can be shockingly misinterpreted if the pe\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 r
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 former\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s intelligence is not guided by his instinct.
\par }\pard \qj \fi328\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx328\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13186552 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
Among all the details of musical interpretation the tempo is of the first importance. With a wrong speed the best performance is more or less distorted. Unfortunately there is almost no limit to misunder\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
standing here. It is not generally realized that many misinterpretations are caused by a wrong perception of the meaning of tempo indications in the music of earlier times. It has not been observed that this meaning has undergone a change in the course of
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 the last century and that our use of tempo indications is fundamentally different from that in Mozart\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s and Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s time. The com\-
poser of today, using the approved and habitual expressions such as Allegro, Andante, Adagio, has a conception of speed d\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 e\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 termined by the beat, }{
\i\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 i.e. }{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
the unit of the bar, as counted by the player or beaten by the conductor. In writing Allegro, 4/4, for example, I intend to indicate a speed of moderately quick crotchets approximately}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  }{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 100}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 -120. }{
\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 This conception of tempo is applica\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
ble to the music of the last three generations, but it is already unreliable with the music of Schumann, Mendelssohn and Chopin, and it is definitely wrong if we try to apply it to the music of Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven and Schubert. I have rarely met a mu
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 s\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 ician who was aware of this fact; and so it can safely be assumed that there are world-renowned conductors who have never noticed it and who}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  }{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 sometimes, with the noble conviction of doing accurately what the composer indicated, are thoroughly mistaken in th
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 eir interpretation of that indication.
\par }\pard \qj \fi328\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx328\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 Let us take an old metronome with Maelzel\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s trade-mark, bearing beside the numbers an odd scale of tempo indications, which may have been made by the inventor himself 120 years ago. This scale, by the way, has been ch\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
anged recently; but generally one is concerned only with the number and nobody bothers about the scale at all. Nevertheless it has a precious historical value, as will be shown at once. \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Adagio\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0  on Maelzel
\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s scale goes from 100 to 126
\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx204\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \loch\af0\dbch\af13\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 an astonishing figur\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 e. We should call such a speed 
\hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Allegro.\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0  But \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Allegro\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
 on the scale extends from 160 to 184, giving the utmost speed of a Vivacissimo or Presto that we can conceive. How is this possible? There can be no question of an Adagio or an Allegro ever having be\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 e
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 n played in such a tremendous hurry. But now I set the metronome beating 120\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 Adagio, according to Maelzel\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s scale\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
 and I play the Adagio cantabile of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 \hich\f0  'Sonate Path\'e9\loch\f0 tique\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 , Op. 13, in the habitual tempo. I find that the beat of my metronome is 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 i\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 n complete accordance with the pace of the accompanying semiquavers. Let us try another experiment, with the first move\-ment of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s sonata in E flat. Op. No. 3 (Allegro, 3/4). I put the metronome to the utmost speed, 2}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid9843668 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 0}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 8. After the rubatos of the f\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 irst sixteen bars it fits the quavers in the bass perfectly, representing the pulse of the whole movement. Now I take another piece of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s with exactly the same tempo indication and time-signature\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the first movement of the sonata in D Major, Op. 2\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 8\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 . It is marked Allegro, 3/4 like the sonata in E}{
\fs36\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid9843668 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  }{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid9843668\charrsid9843668 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 flat}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 . Th}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
e metronome, beating the same speed of 208, coincides exactly now with the crotchets, as it did before with the quavers. This means exactly double the speed.
\par }\pard \qj \fi328\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx328\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid13062919 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 It will be useful to check this experimen\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
t by using the opposite procedure. Let us take three pieces with the same time-signature which every reasonable performer will play at approximately the same speed, as measured by the beat of a crotchet\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
for instance, the first movement of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s sona\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 a, Op. 10, No. 2 (F major, 2/4), the second movement of}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  the sonata, Op. 31, No. 3 A}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid9843668\charrsid9843668 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  flat}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
, 2/4), and the first movement of the sonatina, Op. 49, No. 1 (G minor, 2/4). The tempo of these three movements, as we ordinarily conceive a tempo, is nearly the same;\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  but Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s markings are very different: Allegro, Allegretto vivace, Andante. So the fact that the same tempo indication may express double the speed is confirmed by the other fact that different indications may express the same speed. We have no rig
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 h\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 t to assume that Maelzel was a fool when he drew up his scale, nor that Beethoven was out of his}{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13062919 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  }{
\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 mind when he wrote his tempo indications. The solution of the whole riddle is that they had not the slightest intention of connecting the tempo indications wit
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 h the beat. It is obvious that their perception of tempo was exclusively connected with the main pulse of the music, }{\i\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 i.e. }{
\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 with the actual, audible movement as conceived by the listener. This principle offers a simple explanation of all these apparent absur\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
dities. The method of indicating metronome numbers according to the bigger unit\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the crotchet, the minim, the semibreve\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
seems to have started very early; the practical advantage of this is evident. But there was still no direct connexion between the indica\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 ed unit and the beat, as can be seen in Beethoven\hich\f0 
\rquote \loch\f0 s use of metronome marks. The modern system of tempo marking seems to have been developed by the next generation, and it was generally adopted after 1850.
\par }\pard \qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx357\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid13186552\charrsid13186552 \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 Another modern prejudice which has to be abandoned \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
in performing classical music is our idea that the time-signature can decide the speed. We are used to writing 2/4 or 4/8, C or }{\lang1033\langfe2052\sub\langnp1033\insrsid672049\charrsid7817045 {\*\shppict
