as indications of tempo. Since the sign 4/8 was practically unknown a hundred years ago the comp\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
oser had no opportunity of making clear his intention when he wanted the quaver to be understood as the beat, as the unit of the bar. He simply wrote 2/4. So we have no other way than to apply our common sense: to look at the contents and to judge the tem
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 p\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 o by referring the composer\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s indication to the actual motion of the music. This means, for example, that an Allegro 2/4 with prevalent semiquavers will be almost half as quick, measured by the crotchet, as a piece with the same signature but with prevale\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
n\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 t quavers. The proof of this statement is to be found by comparing the first movement of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s ninth symphony with the first movement of the sixth, and the puzzle of Beethoven\hich\f0 \rquote 
\loch\f0 s tempo and metronome marking in the finale of his \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Eroica\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0  is solved in \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 t\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
he simplest way by applying this principle. Apparently the Presto of the Coda, marked }{\lang1033\langfe2052\langnp1033\insrsid3538972\charrsid3538972 {\*\shppict{\pict{\*\picprop\shplid1030{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}
